Preliminary analytical findings



Our analysis has produced some interesting results:



Airport congestion and departure delays



We found a positive relationship between the number of flights scheduled in an airport-hour and the proportion of delayed departures:



- Airport-hours with 1–2 flights: approximately 15.9% delayed.

- Airport-hours with 41 or more flights: approximately 19.8% delayed.



Average taxi-out time also increases from approximately 14.7 minutes to 19.3 minutes as airport activity increases.



However, the correlation between congestion and departure delay share is only approximately 0.04.



This suggests that congestion is associated with delays, but the relationship is weak and congestion alone explains very little of their variation.



Time-of-day patterns



The time of day appears to have a much stronger relationship with delays:



- 5–6 a.m.: approximately 7% delayed.

- 7–9 p.m.: approximately 28% delayed.



This suggests that delays accumulate throughout the day.



Some of the airport-hours with the highest delay shares include:



- BWI at 9 p.m.: approximately 46%

- DAL at 9 p.m.

- MCO and MDW at 10 p.m.: approximately 44%



Day-of-week patterns



- Fridays and Sundays show the highest delay shares, around 20%.

- Tuesdays and Wednesdays show the lowest, around 17%.



Reported delay causes



Among the reported causes, late aircraft delays account for approximately 40%, followed by carrier delays at approximately 34%. Weather represents only around 5%



We understand that the BTS delay-cause variables refer to arrival delays, so we would like to confirm how best to incorporate this analysis into our departure-congestion study.

